---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>The expedition planning example is the canonical demo. It runs 6 capabilities, 5 events, and 1 workflow entirely on your local machine. Here is the full walkthrough.</p>\n\n      <h2><span class=\"step-num\">1</span> Clone and build</h2>\n      <p>If you have not already, clone the repository and run a full build:</p>\n      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse\ncd traverse\ncargo build</code></pre>\n      <p>This compiles all crates, including the WASM capability modules. The first build takes a few minutes. See <a href=\"/questions/how-do-i-install-traverse.html\">the install guide</a> for prerequisites.</p>\n\n      <h2><span class=\"step-num\">2</span> Inspect the bundle</h2>\n      <p>Verify the build by inspecting the registry bundle. This shows all 6 capabilities indexed with their contract metadata:</p>\n      <pre><code>cargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- bundle inspect examples/expedition/registry-bundle/manifest.json</code></pre>\n      <p>You should see capability IDs, versions, placement targets, and constraint declarations printed to the terminal. If this runs without error, the runtime is healthy.</p>\n\n      <h2><span class=\"step-num\">3</span> Start the Traverse server</h2>\n      <p>There used to be a standalone <code>browser-adapter serve</code> process for this; it was retired once the production server grew the same ordered-update contract natively. Start the production server in a terminal and leave it running:</p>\n      <pre><code>cargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- serve --bind 127.0.0.1:8787</code></pre>\n      <p>The server loads the bundle, initializes the registry, and exposes the HTTP/WebSocket API your frontend talks to — including the browser-subscription endpoint that streams ordered runtime updates.</p>\n\n      <h2><span class=\"step-num\">4</span> Start the React demo</h2>\n      <p>The React browser demo now lives in a separate repo, <a href=\"https://github.com/traverse-framework/App-References\">App-References</a>. Open a second terminal, check that repo out, and point its demo server at the server you started in step 3, e.g. with an <code>--adapter http://127.0.0.1:8787 --port 4173</code>-style flag (confirm the exact flags in that repo's README, since it's updated independently of the core runtime repo). Node.js 20+ is required for this step.</p>\n\n      <h2><span class=\"step-num\">5</span> Submit a request in the browser</h2>\n      <p>Open the URL printed by the React demo server (typically <code>http://127.0.0.1:4173</code>). Fill in the expedition planning form and submit. The frontend streams the request over the server's browser-subscription WebSocket endpoint, which runs the workflow through all 6 capabilities in sequence.</p>\n\n      <h2>What to expect in the terminal</h2>\n      <p>In the server terminal you will see one line per capability execution: the capability ID and the placement target used. After all 6 capabilities complete, the server returns a JSON response to the frontend with the plan output and a full trace artifact.</p>\n\n      <p>The trace artifact is the structured record of the entire run: decision evidence, state progression, the execution and result record, and every event emitted, in order. This is what makes Traverse auditable by design. (Preconditions and postconditions declared in each contract are documentation for reviewers at this stage — the runtime doesn't yet gate execution on them itself.)</p>\n\n      <h2>What you have just seen</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The runtime discover capabilities from the registry bundle</li>\n        <li>WASM binaries executed in a sandboxed environment</li>\n        <li>A trace artifact produced covering the entire workflow</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <p>From here, read the <a href=\"/docs/concepts.html\">concepts documentation</a> to understand what you just observed, or look at the capability source code in <code>crates/traverse-expedition-wasm/</code> to see how capabilities are written.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I run the Traverse expedition example?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Clone the repo, run cargo build, then cargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- bundle inspect examples/expedition/registry-bundle/manifest.json to see the registry. Start the production server with cargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- serve --bind 127.0.0.1:8787. In a second terminal, start the React demo from the separate App-References repo, pointed at that server. Open the browser and submit a request to see the full runtime trace.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How do I run the Traverse expedition example?"}
  description={"Step-by-step walkthrough of running the Traverse expedition planning demo: 6 capabilities, 5 events, 1 workflow, the traverse-cli serve API, and the React frontend."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-run-the-first-example.html"}
  category={"Question"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
